 ## Overview
-Custom Shading Models provide a mechanism for users to define arbitrary illumination calculations for surface points or voxels identified during ray marching, rather than relying on built-in lighting primitives. This family solves the problem of visual fidelity and material expressiveness when standard models (like simple diffuse or Blinn-Phong) are insufficient for specific artistic or scientific requirements. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods like [Distance Color Blending](791-distance-color-blending.md) or [Ray-marched Shadows](1087-ray-marched-shadows.md) by allowing the user to inject custom logic (code or lookup tables) into the shading step, effectively decoupling the geometric traversal from the radiance computation [S1].
+Custom Shading Models provide a mechanism for users to define arbitrary lighting and color computation logic for volume rendering, bypassing standard pre-defined shading tables or simple lookup functions. They solve the problem of limited expressiveness in standard volumetric shading by allowing per-voxel or per-surface-point callbacks that can implement complex material properties, non-physical artistic effects, or specialized lighting approximations [S1]. Unlike sibling methods that focus on specific physical models (like GGX) or specific data types (like voxel grids), this family is defined by the *interface* for customization—typically a callback function or a custom table initialization routine—rather than a single mathematical formula [S1].
 
